	In the following paragraphs I will try to guide you through all the steps on how to use GraphXY.
	The first step that you have to take is to get familiar with the interface. Use the Arrow Keys to select a specific command. Once you have selected the right button press ENTER once. There is always a gray bar on the bottom of your screen that provides you with quick help. Always look at the quick help bar when you are confused on what you have to do.
	So now, as you got familiar with the interface lets go through all the commands. The first thing you have to do is to enter the equation. To enter a new equation, select the New Graph button and press ENTER. Now, you should see a blinking box over one of the fields in which you see y = . You can select any field (they are of different color) by using the Arrow Keys. After you selected a field, press ENTER. You should see a blinking cursor in the field you selected. Now you are ready to type an equation. Type the equation without any special characters. For example, some programs require you to enter the ^ character before you type a power. In this program you type an equation the way you would write it on paper. Whenever you hear a beep when you try to type an equation it means you are entering something wrong. Remember: Use only x as the variable name in the equation you type. For now You can try entering something like this: 2x2-5x+3. This equation can be literally read as two x squared minus five x plus 3. You should notice that the 2 after x automatically became the power of x. Now, that you typed the equation in a field, press Enter again. This will start the graphing of your equation. In a few seconds you should see an equation being graphed on the grid at the right.  
	It is possible to enter 6 different equations because there are 6 fields. You can use the zoom command to zoom in or out. Use the Clear Graph command to clear the graph. If then you want to graph all the entered equation use the Graph All command. The Values command will give you the list of (x, y) plotting points and Roots will give you the roots of an equation.
	More help will be provided as go on the bottom of your screen in the gray quick help box. If you still have questions look in the FAQ or e-mail me at ~15~orsk@hotmail.com.
	